<h2>The case in one view</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Case</th><td>Suhag v. State (NCT of Delhi), BAIL APPLN. 349/2026</td></tr>
  <tr><th>Court</th><td>High Court of Delhi, Justice Sachin Datta</td></tr>
  <tr><th>Decided</th><td>4 November 2026</td></tr>
  <tr><th>FIR</th><td>No. 587/2024, PS Narela Industrial Area, Section 21 NDPS Act; charges framed under Section 21(c) read with Section 29</td></tr>
  <tr><th>Alleged recovery</th><td>280 grams of &ldquo;heroin&rdquo; — commercial quantity</td></tr>
  <tr><th>Outcome</th><td>Regular bail granted under Section 483 BNSS</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>The allegation and the chemistry</h2>
<p>The prosecution&rsquo;s case was possession of 280 grams of heroin, placing the matter in commercial-quantity territory and attracting the twin conditions of Section 37 of the NDPS Act. The chemistry, however, refused to cooperate. The FSL report did not detect diacetylmorphine — the constituent that makes a substance heroin in law — and instead identified 6-monoacetylmorphine, codeine and caffeine.</p>
<p>The prosecution sought to explain the gap as degradation: diacetylmorphine, it argued, breaks down into monoacetylmorphine over time, relying on the 2022 <em>Sheikh Anwar</em> decision. Justice Datta was not persuaded that the explanation could carry the weight of Section 37 at the bail stage. The court held that the FSL report does not support the claim that the recovered substance was heroin, raising a &ldquo;substantial doubt&rdquo; about whether the recovery was of a commercial quantity, and drew on <em>Taslima alias Putti</em> (2026) and <em>Dharmender</em> (2020), where bail followed comparable FSL findings.</p>

<h2>The successive application: what changed</h2>
<p>This was the applicant&rsquo;s second attempt; BAIL APPLN. 94/2025 had been dismissed. The court noted that the earlier order had not examined the FSL discrepancy at all. That omission mattered: a successive bail application is not barred where a material circumstance was never considered, and here the central scientific infirmity in the prosecution case had gone entirely unexamined the first time around.</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice point:</strong> a renewed bail application should identify, in terms, what was not before the court previously — a new FSL analysis, a changed custody period, a stalled trial — rather than re-arguing what was already rejected.</p>
</div>

<h2>Delay: the Mohd. Muslim principle</h2>
<p>The second pillar was time. The applicant had been in custody since 24 June 2024 — some 28 months — while the trial before the Special Judge (NDPS), Rohini Courts, had examined only 5 of 20 prosecution witnesses, with the delay not attributable to the defence. Applying the Supreme Court&rsquo;s ruling in <em>Mohd. Muslim v. State (NCT of Delhi)</em>, the court held that prolonged incarceration with no realistic prospect of early conclusion supports bail notwithstanding Section 37. The applicant&rsquo;s clean NDPS record, two interim bails with timely surrender, and satisfactory jail conduct completed the picture.</p>

<h2>Conditions of release</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li>Personal bond of Rs 50,000 with one surety of the like amount.</li>
  <li>No travel outside the NCT of Delhi or outside India without the court&rsquo;s permission.</li>
  <li>Disclosure of residential address; mobile phone kept operational with location services enabled.</li>
  <li>No contact with prosecution witnesses and no tampering with evidence.</li>
  <li>Attendance on every date of trial; monthly reporting to the IO between 10 and 11 a.m.</li>
  <li>No involvement in any fresh offence.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<h2>Why the decision matters</h2>
<p>Delhi&rsquo;s NDPS docket has seen a steady line of bail orders turning on FSL reports that fail to confirm the alleged contraband — this judgment joins recent orders of the same court on similar findings. Two lessons stand out. For the defence, the FSL report deserves forensic reading in every NDPS brief: the difference between diacetylmorphine and its breakdown products can be the difference between Section 37&rsquo;s bar and liberty. For the prosecution, degradation theories need evidentiary foundation — sampling dates, storage conditions and expert explanation — if they are to answer a facial mismatch at the bail stage. And running beneath both is the constitutional undertone of <em>Mohd. Muslim</em>: the stringency of a special statute does not license indefinite pre-trial detention while a twenty-witness trial inches forward.</p>
